//! World and dungeon pickup flag-id **selection** — the game knowledge "which
//! `getItemFlagId`s are world pickups / legacy-dungeon pickups" (ADR-0010, slice
//! #5).
//!
//! Like [`flag_ids`](super::flag_ids) this holds flag **ids**, never byte offsets;
//! positions resolve per save via `wasm-event-flags` (ADR-0008). Each id is a
//! `getItemFlagId` (NOT a row id — CLAUDE.md: `getItemFlagId = row_id + 7000`
//! holds for most open-world rows but 124 break it, so a row-id convention reads
//! the wrong bit). `ResolvedFlags::tile_pickup` normalises the high-localId form
//! itself.
//!
//! GENERATED, do not hand-edit. Extracted sorted+deduplicated from the reader's
//! MACHINE-CHECKED tables — `src/db/world_pickups.rs` (`flag_id` column) and
//! `src/db/dungeon_pickups.rs` (`event_flag` column). Those two tables partition
//! the primary `ItemLotParam_map` (regulation 1.16.1) exactly and are regenerated
//! by `er-save-reader knowledge gen-world-pickups`; THAT generator's test is the
//! source-of-truth machine-check. These core arrays are a derivative of the
//! reader's committed output and must be regenerated alongside it on a regulation
//! bump. Multiple lot rows can share one `getItemFlagId`, so the unique id counts
//! here are below the reader's row counts (world 2867 rows → 2438 ids; dungeon
//! 2031 rows → 1950 ids) — one flag id resolves to one state.
//!
//! Regenerate (from the ER-save-Reader checkout):
//! ```sh
//! grep -oE 'flag_id: ([0-9]+)' src/db/world_pickups.rs | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sort -n | uniq
//! grep -oE 'event_flag: ([0-9]+)' src/db/dungeon_pickups.rs | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sort -n | uniq
//! ```

